Let $R_{k} \subset \Lambda^{k}(\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3})$ be the span of the single-row $k$-pair states $\bigwedge_{a \in S}(e_{i} \otimes f_{a})$, $i \in \{0, 1\}$, $S \subset \{1,2,3\}$ with $|S| = k$, and identify $R_{k}$ with $\Con_{k}$ by $\bigwedge_{a \in S}(e_{i} \otimes f_{a}) \mapsto e_{i} \otimes f_{S}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $k = 1$ the identification is an isomorphism of $U(2) \times U(3)$-representations: the cell action $A \otimes B$ on the cells and the unit action on $\Con_{1}$ coincide. The pair-level generators of T12 are the one-pair constituents.
\item For $k = 2, 3$ the cell action does not preserve $R_{k}$: the $U(2) \times U(3)$-span of $R_{k}$ under the cell action is $S^{k}\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, of dimension $(k+1)\binom{3}{k}$, that is $(3,\bar{3})$ at $k = 2$ and $(4,1)$ at $k = 3$, with abelian weight $k$; under the unit action $\Con_{k} = (2, \Lambda^{k}\C^{3})$ has dimension $2\binom{3}{k}$ and weight $3 - 2k$. The same basis states, the filled rows among them, carry different representations under the two actions.
\item Consequently the constituent space is not a subrepresentation of the tensor ledger, and the tensor ledger is the Fock space of the pairs, the multi-pair states of the composite counted cell by cell; it is not the space of the composite's constituents.
\end{enumerate}

Assume (C), and list the doublets $Q = \Con_{1}$ and $L = \Con_{3}$ as particles of one handedness. (The arithmetic is that of \cite[Thm.~5.5(3), last clause]{ButlerT12}, with the signs of the two nonzero sums reversed, since T12 conjugates the doublets and this note conjugates the free sector, so the lists are overall conjugates; what is new is only its reading under (C).) There are two ways to list the free sector $\Sp = \Lambda^{\bullet}W$ with them.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If the $\CS$ half of the free sector (grades $0$ and $2$) is listed with the $\CSm{}$ half of the slot sector, where $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}}W$ is the conformal spinor space and $\CSm{} = \Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}}W$ its partner (twistor and dual twistor space), the two chirality halves of $\Sp$, the list is $Q, L, (1)_{0}, (\bar{3})_{-2/3}$, and the conjugates of the free $\CSm{}$ half, $(\bar{3})_{+1/3}$ and $(1)_{+1}$: that is $Q, L, \nu^{c}, u^{c}, d^{c}, e^{c}$, one Standard Model generation with a right-handed neutrino, and all four anomaly conditions vanish.
\item If the $\CSm{}$ half of the free sector is listed with the slot sector's, the list is $Q, L, (3)_{-1/3}, (1)_{-1}$ and the conjugates $(1)_{0}, (3)_{+2/3}$, and the $[SU(3)]^{2}U(1)$ and cubic conditions read $+\tfrac23$ and $-\tfrac49$.
\end{enumerate}
So the consistency of gauging fixes the relative handedness, the bit $\epsilon$ of Hypothesis (C): the chirality half of a free conformal spinor that is listed with the constituents' $\CSm{}$ is $\CS$, that is, $\epsilon = -1$, a slot reverses the half. Equivalently, in physical terms, the free-sector fields are $\nu^{c}$ and $u^{c}$ from $\CS$ and $d_{R}$ and $e_{R}$ from $\CSm{}$, four Weyl fields of the observed charges with no conjugation applied to any of them, and a bare free conformal spinor is the neutrino singlet while a bare conformal spinor in a slot is the antilepton.

Let $u \in \CS\setminus\{0\}$ and define its \emph{annihilator space} $W_u := \{v \in V_\C : c(v)u = 0\}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $W_u$ is a maximal totally isotropic ($3$-dimensional) subspace of $V_\C$: \emph{every} conformal spinor (twistor) is a pure spinor. Moreover $W_u$ determines $u$ up to scale (the joint kernel of $c(W_u)$ is the line $\C u$), so $[u] \mapsto W_u$ is an injection of $\PCS$ into the maximal isotropic subspaces, equivariant for the spin action.
\item For any maximal isotropic complement $U'$ dual to $W_u$, the map $\Lambda^\bullet U' \to \Lambda^\bullet W$, $\xi \mapsto c(\xi)u$, is a linear isomorphism: $u$ is the Fock vacuum of the three-ladder-pair system built on $(W_u, U')$, every state of the module is generated from $u$ by its own pair operators, and $\CS$ (respectively $\CSm$) is what the even (respectively odd) products generate. The pair system is canonical given $u$ up to the choice of dual complement.
\item Ambient vectors act on conformal spinor states as sums of three fermionic creation and annihilation operators; conformal spinors are therefore not \emph{operators} of the pair algebra but its \emph{states}. The annihilator spaces of $\CS$-spinors and of $\CSm$-spinors are disjoint families (no maximal isotropic annihilates both a nonzero even and a nonzero odd spinor).
\end{enumerate}

The maximal hierarchy of three conformal spinors (the three-twistor hierarchy) $\C^{3} \otimes \C^{3}$ has automorphism algebra $\su(3) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, of dimension seventeen, which is not the Standard Model's. The minimal massive one is: mass first exists at two constituents (Fact \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}), so matter's \emph{minimal massive} hierarchy is $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$, two conformal spinors of three pairs each, and its automorphisms form $(U(2) \times U(3))/U(1)$, with Lie algebra
\begin{equation}
\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1),
\end{equation}
of dimension $4 + 9 - 1 = 12 = 3 + 8 + 1$: the Standard Model Lie algebra exactly. The global group is determined by Theorem \claimref{standard-model.global-form-quotient-determined}.

The global gauge group of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite} is determined exactly. The kernel of the $(U(2) \times U(3))$-action on $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ is $K = \{(\lambda I_{2}, \lambda^{-1} I_{3})\} \cong U(1)$, and
\begin{equation}
(U(2) \times U(3))/K \;\cong\; S(U(2) \times U(3)) \;\cong\; \bigl(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1)\bigr)/\mathbb{Z}_{6}:
\end{equation}
the framework's group is the smallest of the Standard Model's global forms, the full $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ quotient, not the naive product and not either intermediate quotient. Three consequences. (i) $S(U(2) \times U(3))$ is exactly the subgroup of $SU(5)$ preserving the splitting $\C^{5} = \C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}$, and none of the three proper covers $(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1))/\Gamma$, $\Gamma \subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_{6}$, acts faithfully through it; so the framework's global form is the one that grand unification's embedding requires, and the trace ratio $3/8$ of the section ``Unification and the desert'' refers to the same normalisation from the other side. (ii) The representations of the group are confined to the $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ charge lattice: on a representation of the covering product with colour triality $t \in \mathbb{Z}_{3}$, weak parity $\delta \in \mathbb{Z}_{2}$ (the class of the $SU(2)$ content: $1$ on a doublet, $0$ on a singlet or triplet) and abelian weight $q \in \mathbb{Z}$ (the integer such that the $U(1)$ factor $\lambda$ acts by $\lambda^{q}$), descent to the quotient holds if and only if $2t + 3\delta + q \equiv 0 \pmod 6$; writing $Y = q/6$ this is the condition that $t/3 + \delta/2 + Y$ be an integer. The content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency} lies on it (each of its representations is built as a representation of $S(U(2) \times U(3))$), and the lattice check is printed there. (iii) A structural stance, registered: the lattice forbids representations off it, so a confirmed discovery of, for example, a fractionally charged colour singlet would refute the identification of the gauge group with the automorphism group of minimal massive compositeness, a stance that current searches test \cite{PDG2024}. The stance inherits the conditionality of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite}'s premise and is flagged at that level.

Under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, with $Y$ the hypercharge just defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Unconditional.) The pair-level generators, the cells of the tensor state space, transform as $(2, 3)$ with abelian weight $q = 1$, so $Y = 1/6$: the quark doublet's representation. A single pair has one unit of hypercharge.
\item (Under Hypothesis (S).) The states with one slot mode occupied and the pair modes in an $SU(3)$-invariant configuration are $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{0}W$, weight $3$ ($Y = +1/2$, total mode number one, odd), and $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{3}W$, weight $-3$ ($Y = -1/2$, total mode number four, even); both transform as $(2, 1)$. Without (S) neither exists in the state space, whose only row-filling states form the quadruplet $(4,1)$ of weight $+3$.
\item (Unconditional as a computation on the Fock space of a single conformal spinor, with the pair-slot factor acting alone; that Fock space is not a subspace of the tensor state space, and under (S) it is the row $j = 0$.) In the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector) $\Lambda^{\bullet}W = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3}$, the grade-$k$ states transform as $\Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, that is as $1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$, with weight $q = -2k$: $Y = 0, -1/3, -2/3, -1$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, under (S), the fermion content of the composite at the particle handedness (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}) is
\begin{equation}
(2,3)_{1/6} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2},
\end{equation}
quark doublet plus lepton doublet, with colour neutrality of the lepton the statement that a state with its pair modes full is an $SU(3)$ singlet: a lepton is a complete conformal spinor, one slot with the pair system full, while a quark is one slot with one pair.

Under $\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, with the abelian charge the pair number, the full Fock space of the minimal massive hierarchy decomposes as
\begin{equation}
\Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}) = (1,1) \oplus (2,3) \oplus \bigl[(3,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,6)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(4,1) \oplus (2,8)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(3,3) \oplus (1,\bar{6})\bigr] \oplus (2,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,1),
\end{equation}
graded by pair number $0$ through $6$; and the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector, massless) decomposes as $\Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3} = 1 \oplus 3 \oplus \bar{3} \oplus 1$, graded $0$ through $3$.
